P3 Search-aware clean v2: duplicate-record source fix + collective-safe padding
guard (2026-08-23).

Part 1 -- duplicate-record source fix. The 2026-08-22 engineering smoke
(fsdp6-b66-n5-s0-20260822a) showed 4 duplicate (uid, traj_uid, env_step)
identities in the training batch (804 audit records for 800 real rollout
records). Root cause: adjust_batch(..., mode="copy") copy-padded random REAL
rows to reach the DP-divisible batch size (800 % 6 = 2 -> 4 copies),
double-counting those 4 trajectories' rewards and policy gradients and
breaking S6 state threading.

Fix (at the earliest assembly point, adjust_batch):
  - "copy" mode now appends SYNTHETIC padding rows: is_padding=True, unique
    uid/traj_uid "__pad__{k}", env_step=-1, search_v1=None (v2 reward manager
    skips them; a real record missing search_v1 still raises), zero rewards ->
    each padding row is its own size-1 GRPO group -> advantage exactly 0.0.
  - The padding rows keep ONE attended token at prompt position 0: the actor's
    unpad (attention_mask.nonzero()) must not produce an empty sequence - an
    all-zero row crashes the HF forward ("cannot reshape tensor of 0 elements
    into shape [1, 0, -1, 128]"; observed in actor_rollout_compute_log_prob on
    the 2026-08-23a smoke). The response part stays all-zero -> response_mask
    all-zero -> zero policy loss; loss_mask (multi-turn batches) is zeroed so
    copied row-0 masks cannot leak a real gradient.
  - dp_actor.update_policy handles micro-batches whose response_mask sums to 0
    (token-mean aggregation 0/0 would be NaN; contribution is exactly 0).
  - assert_unique_training_records() fail-closed invariant: (uid, traj_uid,
    env_step) uniquely identifies a training record (each env step emits
    exactly one record type); enforced in ray_trainer AFTER adjust_batch and
    BEFORE reward placement and advantage computation; raises with both record
    indices on any duplicate identity.
  - the v2 reward manager (episode.py) skips is_padding rows (no reward
    placement, no accounting), stamps "version": "v2" into the
    search_v1_episode / search_v1_group aggregates, and its traj accounting
    sanity counts only non-padding records.

Part 2 -- collective-safe padding guard (deadlock root cause, 2026-08-23).
The first guard version SKIPPED padding micro-batches with `continue`. That is
per-rank divergent: _balance_batch dispatches the batch to DP ranks via
get_seqlen_balanced_partitions (Karmarkar-Karp), so the 1-token padding rows
land in DIFFERENT ranks' partitions and ranks execute DIFFERENT numbers of
FSDP forwards/backwards. Their NCCL collective sequences diverge and deadlock
at the mini-batch boundary: ranks that finish their mini-batch first enter
_optimizer_step's grad-norm ALLREDUCE while ranks still in the loop issue the
next forward's embedding ALLGATHER. Observed as a DETERMINISTIC hang on runs
20260823b and 20260823c (identical watchdog SIGABRT ~30 min after onset; 4
ranks hung on ALLREDUCE, 2 ranks on _ALLGATHER_BASE). The 20260822a night run
was unaffected: its 804-row batch was divisible by 6, so adjust_batch never
padded and no rank skipped anything.

Fix: every rank runs the SAME forward+backward for a padding micro-batch, with
a loss that is connected to the model (FSDP post-backward hooks fire uniformly,
so all-gather/reduce-scatter sequences stay identical across ranks) but exactly
zero: log_prob.masked_fill(all-True mask, 0.0).sum() == 0.0 for ANY log_prob
content (even -inf), so the padding rows contribute no gradient at all. The
1-token forward is the same path the log_prob/ref phases already ran on these
rows (proven safe on runs b/c).

Covered by tests/test_p3_v2_duplicate_fix.py (D1..D12 + zero-loss guard tests)
and the updated scripts/check_p3_v2_smoke.py (S5 version key, S6 leak not
recomputable, S7 active-token advantage representation).

